.TH RATELIMIT 1 "2026-10-30" "ratelimit 1.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ratelimit \- bidirectional, multi-rule TCP/UDP rate-limiting proxy
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ratelimit
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.B \-\-src
\fIHOST:PORT[\-PORT]\fR
.B \-\-dst
\fIHOST:PORT[\-PORT]\fR
[\fI\-\-rate\fR N] ...
.br
.B ratelimit
.B \-\-config
\fIFILE\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ratelimit
is a small proxy that listens on one or more source addresses and
forwards traffic to a destination, enforcing per-connection bandwidth
limits with a token-bucket algorithm in both directions independently.
.PP
It supports TCP and UDP, IPv4 and IPv6, multiple simultaneous rules,
port-range mapping, per-source-IP connection limits with automatic
blacklisting of abusers, idle-connection timeouts, a live monitor and
hot configuration reloads on
.BR SIGHUP .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-src =\fIHOST:PORT[\-PORT]\fR
Address to listen on. Repeatable; each
.B \-\-src
must be followed by a
.BR \-\-dst .
IPv6 addresses use bracket notation: \fB[::1]:8080\fR. A port range
\fBHOST:P1\-P2\fR is also accepted (see \fBPORT RANGES\fR).
An empty host (e.g. \fB:8080\fR) binds all interfaces, dual-stack.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dst =\fIHOST:PORT[\-PORT]\fR
Destination to forward to. Hostnames are resolved at connection time.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-rate =\fIVALUE\fR
Rate limit value (default: 1024). Applies to the rule being defined, or
retroactively to the last single-port rule created.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-unit =\fIkbytes|packets\fR
Rate unit (default: kbytes). \fIkbytes\fR limits bytes per second;
\fIpackets\fR limits datagrams/chunks per second.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-proto =\fItcp|udp\fR
Protocol (default: tcp).
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name =\fINAME\fR
Rule name for the next \fB\-\-src\fR/\fB\-\-dst\fR pair (or of the last
single-port rule created).
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config =\fIFILE\fR
Load configuration from
.IR FILE .
Rules from the file and from the command line are merged. Required for
.B SIGHUP
hot reloads.
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-save\-config =\fIFILE\fR
Write the effective configuration to
.I FILE
and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Show the live connection monitor.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interval =\fISECS\fR
Monitor refresh interval in seconds (default: 1).
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-max\-conn =\fIN\fR
Maximum simultaneous connections per source IP, per rule (default: 200).
An IP rejected three times in a row is blacklisted for 300 seconds.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-timeout =\fISECS\fR
Idle connection timeout in seconds (default: 60). A connection is closed
only when no data flowed in \fIeither\fR direction for that long.
0 disables the timeout.
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-log\-level =\fI0\-3\fR
0=ERR, 1=WARN, 2=INFO (default), 3=DBG.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show version and exit.
.PP
Command-line options take precedence over the configuration file.
Per-rule options (\fB\-r/\-u/\-p/\-n/\-m/\-t\fR) attach to the
\fB\-\-src\fR/\fB\-\-dst\fR pair they follow (or to the pair being defined
when placed before its \fB\-\-dst\fR). Duplicate listeners (same protocol,
source host and source port) are detected after all options have been
applied and dropped with a warning; the same source port may therefore be
defined once for TCP and once for UDP.
.SH PORT RANGES
.B \-\-src
and
.B \-\-dst
accept port ranges of the form \fIHOST:P1\-P2\fR (max 1024 ports).
Both ranges must span the same number of ports, or the destination may
be a single port shared by all source ports. Every pair expands into an
independent rule with its own listener and rate limiter:
.PP
.nf
.RS
ratelimit \-s 0.0.0.0:8000\-8009 \-d 10.0.0.1:9000\-9009 \-r 512 \-n web
.RE
.fi
.PP
maps 8000\(->9000, 8001\(->9001, ..., 8009\(->9009. Expanded rules are named
\fIbase\-port\fR (\fBweb\-8000\fR, \fBweb\-8001\fR, ...). The same syntax
works in the configuration file:
.PP
.nf
.RS
[web]
src_host = 0.0.0.0
src_port = 8000\-8009
dst_host = 10.0.0.1
dst_port = 9000\-9009
rate     = 512
.RE
.fi
.SH RATE LIMITING
A token bucket is applied per connection and direction. In
.I kbytes
mode the bucket refills at \fIrate \(mu 1024\fR bytes/s with a burst
capacity of 4 \(mu that value; in
.I packets
mode it refills at \fIrate\fR tokens/s. TCP traffic is delayed
(backpressure); UDP datagrams that find an empty bucket are dropped and
counted in the monitor.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.BR SIGINT ", " SIGTERM
Graceful shutdown: listeners stop, in-flight connections get ~1 second
to finish, then the process exits.
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload the configuration file (requires
.BR \-\-config ).
The new configuration is validated before anything is torn down; an
invalid file is ignored and the current state is kept. Existing
connections keep running until they close naturally (downtime-free
reload); new connections follow the new rules.
.SH SECURITY
Per-source-IP connection limits, automatic blacklisting (300 s after
3 consecutive rejections), a global connection cap
(\fBglobal_max_connections\fR), bounded outbound connect timeouts (5 s),
idle timeouts, fixed-size buffers and session tables,
.B SIGPIPE
suppression and hardened build flags (PIE, RELRO, FORTIFY,
stack-protector).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/ratelimit/ratelimit.conf
Default configuration file used by the systemd unit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success (clean shutdown or \fB\-\-save\-config\fR/\fB\-\-help\fR/\fB\-\-version\fR),
1 on invalid arguments, invalid configuration, or if no listener could be started.
.SH EXAMPLES
Single 512 kB/s TCP proxy:
.PP
.nf
.RS
ratelimit \-\-src 0.0.0.0:8080 \-\-dst 127.0.0.1:80 \-\-rate 512
.RE
.fi
.PP
UDP DNS relay at 500 packets/s plus a TLS proxy, with the live monitor:
.PP
.nf
.RS
ratelimit \\
  \-\-src 0.0.0.0:5353 \-\-dst 10.0.0.1:53  \-\-rate 500 \-\-unit packets \-\-proto udp \\
  \-\-src [::]:8443    \-\-dst 10.0.0.1:443 \-\-rate 2048 \-\-verbose
.RE
.fi
.PP
Same port for both protocols (DNS-style):
.PP
.nf
.RS
ratelimit \\
  \-\-src 0.0.0.0:53 \-\-dst 10.0.0.53:53 \-\-proto tcp \-\-rate 512 \-\-name dns\-tcp \\
  \-\-src 0.0.0.0:53 \-\-dst 10.0.0.53:53 \-\-proto udp \-\-rate 2000 \-\-unit packets \-\-name dns\-udp
.RE
.fi
